Development prototype (not an installed v2 release):

- Deliberate v2 linker-script correction: numeric arguments must consume the
  whole token. Reject 8000h, $C000x, 1e3 and a bare $ rather than silently using
  the numeric prefix as v1 does. Existing valid numeric spellings are unchanged.
- Add the explicit, non-installed xasm-v2 target for plain pure-binary assembly.
  It uses the existing source frontend and the owned v2 value/layout backend.
  Installed xasm/xlnk, v1 objects and analysis formats remain unchanged.
- Apply the approved v2 source rules: forward string data and use-site character
  maps, acyclic late storage spans, and fixed-point narrowing on the binary path.
  Strings are resolved before integer identities: S+0 concatenates when S is a
  string; string *1, /1 and 1* operands are rejected.
- Reject explicit-address .label name = address with a located error; use an
  equate for a fixed address. Ordinary .label name remains supported. Also reject
  $ in type bodies and expansion-time $ when preceding layout is unresolved.
- Keep sizeof(label) at source evaluation time, with the approved local-forward
  kind checks and rejection of imported or still-deferred string extents.
  If a forward data operand's kind or string length changes after sizeof counts
  it, reject at the sizeof use instead of publishing a stale size. This can
  reject v1-accepted programs using reassignment or .undef; numeric changes,
  same-length strings and values captured at the data line remain valid.
  These changes belong only to the prototype until review and staged cutover.
- Reject strings built from the containing label's own sizeof with a located
  size-unknown error, even without reassignment or .undef. For example,
  Tbl: .db sizeof(Tbl)+"A" no longer emits "1A" with a stale one-byte size.
  Numeric sizeof operands and strings using unrelated label sizes remain valid.
  This deliberate v2 change preserves existing outputs when assembly fails.
- Source-time $ now uses a known final PC: after lda $10 it is 2, not v1's
  provisional 3. This can change conditional expansion and .equ/.define/=
  captures. Unresolved instruction or data widths instead give a located error.
- Map every string operand in .char with the captured map. For H=1, I=0,
  .char "HI","HI" emits 01 00 01 00; v1 left the second string unmapped.

Version 1.9.12:

- Define integer expression arithmetic consistently as signed 32-bit values
  with wrapping results in the assembler, linker, listings and analysis.
  Addition, subtraction, multiplication, unary minus and left shift discard
  bits above bit 31. Division truncates toward zero; $80000000 / -1 wraps
  to $80000000, with remainder zero. Shift counts must be 0 through 31.
  This removes signed-overflow and invalid-shift undefined behavior.
- Deliberate compatibility change: link-time calculations now wrap at 32 bits
  instead of using the host's long width. For example, ($40000000*8)-1 is -1,
  including with external-symbol or label operands. Comparisons and range
  checks use the wrapped signed value. These rules also apply to older objects;
  the object encoding is unchanged. Listings and analysis leave zero-divisor
  expressions unevaluated rather than reporting a fabricated value of zero.
- Reject $ in .equ, .define and = after storage has left the first-pass
  position uncertain, instead of capturing a provisional address. Use a label
  for the final address, or define counts before allocating storage. The check
  includes deferred field padding and user-type storage and is conservative:
  defining a count later does not recalculate the preceding layout.
- Reject PC-relative .org after unresolved storage with a located error,
  preserving existing outputs. Use an absolute origin or define storage counts
  before use. Known relative origins remain supported, code and data segments
  are independent, and an absolute origin restores a known position.
- Preserve listing, instruction-record and object schemas. Remaining first-pass
  PC limitations and future format changes are tracked in TODO.

Version 1.9.11:

- Preserve dotted member names during constant substitution. Global .equ,
  .define and = constants no longer replace field tokens or cause assertion
  aborts. Base expressions and array indexes still resolve in the caller's
  scope, including nested indexes, aliases and instruction analysis.
- Deliberate compatibility correction: px .equ py no longer redirects p.px
  to p.py, and a global alias cannot make a nonexistent member valid.
  Colliding programs can now emit corrected bytes, fail with a located member
  diagnostic, or assemble correctly where the global substitution rejected
  them before. Programs without member-name collisions retain their output.
- Order tied xref uses by source filename, line, column and collection order
  after the existing address, segment, output-offset and symbol keys.
  JSON, text and CSV retain every reference and duplicate in the same order
  across platforms; only previously unspecified ties change.
- Preserve listing, instruction-record and object schemas. Remaining
  limitations and separately planned consumer migrations are tracked in TODO.

Version 1.9.10:

- Report listing v2 instruction addressing modes from the final opcode.
  Late zero-page shortening now agrees with the bytes and instruction records.
  Operandless asl, lsr, rol and ror report accumulator rather than implied.
- Exclude dotted and scoped member names from global xref references, data
  references and instruction-record reference lists. Preserve base names and
  genuine references inside indexes, along with expressions and source locations.
- Apply the scoped-name rule to enums and records too: Color::Green references
  Color, while bare Green references Green and retains its enum-member binding.
  Names used only as scoped members no longer create global xref symbols.
- Preserve assembled binaries, object files, text listings and v1 listing
  metadata. No listing, instruction-record or object-format migration is included.
  Remaining limitations, including tied-reference ordering, are tracked in TODO.

Version 1.9.9:

- Restore pure-binary procedure and variable addresses lost in 1.7.0,
  including forward calls, recursive procedures, data-segment variables and
  indexed operands. Nested member indexes now also work in pure-binary output.
- Resolve dotted references inside array indexes in the caller's scope,
  including nested indexes and forward aliases. Valid object expressions
  retain their relocations; invalid members produce located errors and
  preserve existing output.
- Resolve generated element sizes in indexed instruction analysis. Separate
  records, xref instructions and v2 listings agree with the assembled binary.
- Deliberate compatibility change: late immediate and indexed-indirect byte
  operands must fit -128..255 in pure-binary output, matching the linker.
  Previously warned-and-truncated values outside that range now fail without
  replacing output. Zero-page addresses remain 0..255. Literal truncation,
  word-operand warnings and branch-displacement checks retain their behavior.
- Preserve binary object formats and listing/instruction-record schemas.
  Remaining limitations, including late-shortened instruction mode metadata,
  phantom member names in xref and word-operand overflow policy, are in TODO.

Version 1.9.8:

- Bind local-label references in saved .equ, .define and = expressions to the
  definition's scope, including macro namespaces. Forward uses now resolve
  in operands and storage counts.
- Deliberate compatibility change: later cross-scope alias uses now refer to
  the definition's local label, rather than the use site's. They can produce
  different bytes or fail if the local is absent from the definition's scope.
  Repeated .equ/.define definitions across scopes can warn and retain the
  first definition; = reassignment binds its replacement in the new scope.
- Reject = assignments to non-constant symbols before evaluating the
  replacement, preserving original definitions and existing output. This
  deliberately rejects formerly accepted assignments to labels, procedures,
  types, macros, variables and external symbols. Constant reassignment and
  enum declarations retain their behavior.
- Show resolved local-alias values in text symbol tables and, for .equ and
  .define, in cross-reference symbol definitions.
- Extend regression coverage for late-storage extents, zero-fill reconstruction
  and analysis offsets. Version 2 listings already describe that fill; text
  and version 1 continue omitting storage bytes by design.
- Preserve binary object formats and listing/instruction-record schemas.
  Remaining storage-layout limitations and the policy for separately planned
  future format migrations with Nesrev and other consumers are in TODO.

Version 1.9.7:

- Evaluate $ in cross-reference data expressions at each operand's address,
  including successive operands in a directive and addresses past $FFFF.
  Valid programs that previously failed with --xref-data now assemble, and
  reported emitted values match the binary. Binary and object bytes are
  unchanged.
- Free replaced constant definitions on = reassignment, including loops,
  while preserving captured operand values and original definition locations.
  Borrowed definitions belonging to other symbol kinds remain untouched.
- Use unsigned keys when sorting linker data labels, removing undefined
  signed shifting while preserving allocation priority and linked addresses.
- Extend regression coverage for reassignment ownership and incomplete
  analysis: a test-only collector wrapper verifies that the publication guard
  preserves every existing output even when the binary writer succeeds.
- Preserve object formats, listing schemas and instruction-record schemas.
  Remaining limitations are tracked in TODO, including assignments to
  non-constant symbols and FCEUX ROM diagnostics for unresolved storage.

Version 1.9.6:

- Preserve negative 32-bit object expressions and exported constants on
  64-bit hosts. In-range negative relocated operands now link, and negative
  byte/word data no longer produce spurious truncation warnings. Compact
  positive encodings and the object format are unchanged.
- Define >> consistently as a logical 32-bit shift in constant folding,
  binary evaluation, listings, analysis and linking. Counts must be 0..31;
  a zero shift retains the signed value. For example, (-2)>>1 is $7FFFFFFF.
  This deliberately changes formerly sign-filling negative label expressions
  in binary output and some previously successful links. Listing bytes,
  symbol values and instruction records now match the emitted binary.
- Check lower and upper bounds on relocated linker instruction operands.
  Byte/word data outside their signed/unsigned ranges warn before truncation,
  with negative values in decimal and wide positive values in full hex.
- Keep located writer diagnostics for unevaluable data/instruction analysis
  values, while protecting existing output from incomplete publication.
  ROM export with unknown storage extents remains an exception: it can suppress
  the diagnostic listing and a late negative count's located range error.
- Avoid cloning unresolved arithmetic operands in x+0, 0+x, x*1, 1*x and x/1.
  This fixes crashes during zero-page optimization and late storage-size
  evaluation without changing completed expressions' bytes or addressing modes.
- Preserve listing and instruction-record schemas. Known limitations remain
  in TODO, including forward-storage link layout and omitted listing fill
  bytes, ROM-storage diagnostics, and current-PC data-reference analysis.

Version 1.9.5:

- Reject recursive sizeof layouts and label-storage cycles with diagnostics
  instead of crashes or repeated expansion. Structures and unions cannot use
  their own unfinished size; this also rejects the field-order-dependent
  provisional union sizes previously accepted. Completed nested types and
  repeated independent size queries remain supported.
- Validate mask namespaces, members and record layouts without crashing.
  Resolve known named widths in the declaration's constant scope, preserving
  capture before reassignment even when a field shadows the constant. Forward
  aliases to masks now produce the correct bits instead of zero. Widths must
  still be known when the record is declared.
- Keep procedure definitions alive after flattening their bodies, preventing
  use-after-free in instruction xref and separate instruction records. Preserve
  declaration locations, existing diagnostics and linked bytes. Linux CI now
  runs the procedure lifetime regressions with instrumented xasm and xlnk.
- Preserve listing and instruction-record schemas. Pure-binary resolution of
  procedure and variable addresses remains unsupported; explicit body labels
  work. Other known limitations remain in TODO, including repeated diagnostics
  on invalid size expressions.

Version 1.9.4:

- Resolve named counts in scoped S::member offsets before binary evaluation
  and object encoding. Diagnose direct and mutual field-offset cycles while
  allowing references to earlier members of the same type. Cache completed
  integer offsets per evaluation to avoid repeated dependency-tree expansion.
- Explicit S::member expressions capture known reassignable counts at the use
  site, matching sizeof(T). Uninitialized storage and other layout paths can
  still use final values; reassignment does not give a type one fixed layout.
- Reject unsupported object expressions, operators and statements instead
  of publishing unloadable objects. Report the 16384-expression limit once per
  unit, preserve previous output on failure, and retain duplicate reuse when
  the table is full. Forward local aliases and nested dotted indexes remain
  unsupported and tracked for earlier resolution.
- Reject late zero-page operands outside 0..255 in pure-binary output, matching
  the linker. Validate the emitted opcode, including indexed zero-page modes.
  Force a valid absolute encoding with .w when shortening would invalidate an
  operand; literal bytes, signed immediates and branches retain their behavior.
- Document remaining limits, including unresolved link-time expression-tree
  growth, pure sizeof layout recursion, mask-reduction crashes and late
  immediate/indirect operand differences between binary and linked output.

Version 1.9.3:
- Resolve named constants in copied structure-size expressions, so indexed
  member references use the correct stride in objects, including nested and
  external instances.
- Validate late binary storage counts and reject extents that change after
  address calculation, preserving previous output on failure. Keep unresolved
  binary storage separate so each count uses its own PC and range check.
- Preserve late zero-length omitted and uninitialized structure-array fields,
  including nested arrays and forward constants. Reject late negative counts
  in objects even across nested fields and merged storage. Known positive
  omitted-array counts retain direct expansion.
- Apply the positive-count rule for standalone storage and the outer count of
  .T[n] to late counts in both modes. Some late zero counts accepted by 1.9.2
  are now rejected; explicitly initialized user-type arrays require positive
  counts too.
- Recalculate code labels and unit origins after linker zero-page shortening,
  repeating layout until instruction sizes settle. Preserve forced-wide
  instructions and fixed label addresses; reject final zero-page operands
  outside 0..255 without publishing output.
- Explicit sizeof(T) now captures a known reassignable = count at its use site
  instead of its final value. Other layout paths can still use the final value.
- Document PC-dependent limitations: .equ $ and = $ can capture provisional
  addresses after deferred storage. Outer omitted-array remainders and nested
  field counts can evaluate $ at different positions in binary and object
  modes; binary layouts can differ from 1.9.2. Use labels or fixed counts where
  matching layouts are required.
- Preserve listing schemas and instruction-record schema version 3. Remaining
  defects and the extra cost of nested late omitted-array counts are in TODO.

Version 1.9.2:
- Validate deferred counts in direct storage, omitted fields and built-in array
  padding before storage merging and object encoding, avoiding dropped storage
  and spurious 256-byte fills. Preserve valid zero-length fields and storage
  grouping in initialized and uninitialized structures.
- Retain labels reached through forward count aliases before unused-label
  removal, so listing and analysis options no longer change assembly results
  or unused-label warnings.
- Diagnose invalid dotted references and unresolved storage expressions without
  crashes or invalid objects, preserving existing output files on failure.
- Resolve forward constant aliases in ordinary operands and sizeof label data,
  with cycle detection and validation of the symbols at the end of a chain.
- Accept previously defined named counts in unions, in the constant scope,
  and avoid cascaded errors after an invalid union declaration.
- Cache completed integer aliases within each evaluation and remove repeated
  statement-list scans when expanding aggregates and merging adjacent data.
- Emit numeric .char data and relocations as bytes in objects. Preserve string
  character maps and match binary-mode out-of-range truncation warnings.
- Preserve listing schemas and instruction-record schema version 3. Deferred
  zero counts on omitted structure-array fields remain unsupported, and union
  dimensions must be known at declaration time. Other known limitations,
  including address-dependent counts and local-label aliases, remain in TODO.

Version 1.9.1:
- Validate deferred counts of explicitly initialized user-type array fields
  before storage merging and object encoding. Reject zero and negative counts,
  including counts defined after an instance, without replacing existing output.
- Resolve aliases and unary expressions in these counts, detect circular
  definitions, and preserve values already known when an instance is expanded.
- Remove zero padding before object encoding to avoid spurious 256-byte fills.
  Preserve adjacent storage grouping and listing source locations for previously
  correct inputs, including nested arrays and omitted subfields.
- Report field-specific errors for invalid named counts, matching literal-count
  diagnostics without cascading errors after an invalid initializer kind.
- Preserve listing schemas and instruction-record schema version 3. Other
  storage-count paths, ordinary-operand aliases and named union counts remain
  tracked in TODO, along with resolver and aggregate-instance performance work.

Version 1.9.0:
- Preserve the declared size of explicitly initialized user-type array fields
  in structures and unions: initialize the first element and zero-fill the rest.
  Improve invalid-count diagnostics and avoid cascaded padding errors.
- Continue assembly after empty initializer expansions and taken conditional
  branches, preserving following labels and data and avoiding assertion aborts
  after repeated initializer errors. Release unused structure fill expressions.
- Add opt-in --listing-version=2 for pure-binary JSON/NDJSON listings, with
  segment, byte-count and emission metadata, compressed storage fill, and
  emitting spans that partition the binary. Diagnostic listings do not claim
  bytes from a previous output file.
- Account for numeric .CHAR data in listing v2 and warn when an explicit
  listing version is supplied without a listing destination. Document offset
  joins and CPU-address wraparound.
- Preserve default listing v1 output and instruction-record schema version 3.
  Named and deferred user-type array count limitations remain tracked in TODO.

Version 1.8.2:
- Restore documented omitted fields in structure, union and record initializers.
  Leading, middle, trailing and consecutive omissions retain their positions
  and default to zero, including nested initializers and macro expansion.
- Correct padding for omitted user-defined fields and anonymous unions. Fix
  assertion aborts for .CHAR storage, omitted .CHAR fields and short strings.
- Diagnose unsupported gaps inside built-in array initializers explicitly.
- Add 16 regression tests covering emitted bytes, linked objects, character
  maps and label/listing/xref addresses after padding. Output schemas are unchanged.

Version 1.8.1:
- Correct structured-listing binary offsets after code-segment storage and
  non-emitting data-segment initialized data or .INCBIN. CPU addresses,
  assembled bytes and diagnostics are unchanged.
- Add 28 regression subcases covering storage widths, empty storage records,
  segment switches and continuations in JSON/NDJSON and debug/non-debug modes.
- Keep listing schema version 1 and instruction-record schema version 3.
  Non-emitting data-segment rows still lack per-row emission metadata; see TODO.

Version 1.8.0:
- Incompatible analysis-format change: instruction records now use schema
  version 3, with a shared files table and integer file indices in every span.
  Older records schemas are no longer emitted; consumers must update.
- Add explicit memory-access facts and signed additive operand terms with
  per-use symbol bindings, including reassigned constants.
- Correct access classification across cross-references and analyses: model
  read-modify-write sites as both reads and writes, BIT/CMP as reads, pointer
  operands as pointer reads, and immediate operands as immediate references.
  See XASM_INSTRUCTION_RECORDS_V2_SPEC.md for the complete migration contract.
- Reduce instruction-record size and serialization cost through shared paths,
  faster span output, and buffering of the standalone records file. Add a
  default performance-regression limit to the real-project comparison tool.
- Install generated xasm/xlnk man pages and a worked analysis-feature guide;
  include them in the tested source distribution.

Version 1.7.0:
- Add FCEUX ROM/RAM debugger name export using physical PRG page offsets,
  independent of xref filters, with optional 16 KiB NROM mirroring.
- Add versioned instruction/operand records, standalone instruction sidecars,
  data-directive references, and content-hashed dependency manifests.
- Add structured listings, xref summaries, data-reference and index-pattern
  analyses, data consumers/coverage, raw-address audits, and binary comparison.
- Preserve output destinations on failed sidecar writes, closes, and renames;
  report listing and stdout analysis errors.
- Improve parser, AST removal, symbol lookup, and JSON serialization costs.
- Check linker writes and closes, preserving existing files on failure, including
  scripts that switch output destinations.
- Grow symbol scope storage safely and reject failed symbol-name allocations.
- Reject malformed/truncated object files and malformed linker scripts; clean up
  incomplete parses and test allocation failures and excessive nesting.
- Include tests and feature specifications in source archives. Test unpacked
  archives and installed tools on Linux/macOS, with sanitizer checks on Linux.

Version 1.6.1:
- Modernize autotools
Version 1.6.0:
- Added support for .w ("wide") mnemonic suffix to force 16-bit address encoding. This can
  be used to disable the default zero page optimization of instructions. Example:
  lda.w $006B
